    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Bottom Line: Is This a Good Offer?\n\nIt is a **standard baseline offer** for a Google L6 PM in the Bay Area, but **it is not strong enough to leave a role where you are happy and thriving.**\n\n* **Base salary is a pay cut:** Google is offering $235,000, which is lower than your current $240,000 base and sits well below their posted cap of $271,000.\n* **No sign-on bonus:** Walking away from $200,000 in unvested Meta RSUs and accrued bonus without a sign-on bonus leaves significant money on the table.\n* **Total Compensation upside is marginal:** Year 1 total comp at Google would be ~$552,000 (if evenly vested) to ~$638,000 (if front-loaded), compared to your current Meta baseline of ~$526,000. \n\n**Recommendation: Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away yet.** You hold maximum leverage: Google recruited you, you are content at Meta, and you have proven IC6 scope. **You should counter firmly.**\n\n---\n\n### 2. Deep-Dive Analysis of the Offer\n\n#### A. Equity & Vesting Schedule (Verify the Fine Print)\n* **Standard Google Schedule:** Google typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial GSUs (33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%). \n  * If your $1.08M grant vests under 33/33/22/12, your Year 1 equity is **$356,400** (not $270,000).\n  * If it is a flat 25% across 4 years (as the recruiter modeled), it is **$270,000/yr**.\n* **The \"Cliff\" in Year 3/4:** Front-loaded grants drop sharply in Years 3 and 4. Even with $250k/year in refreshes, your comp would plateau or drop unless you receive high-tier performance multipliers.\n\n#### B. Base and Bonus\n* **Base Salary:** At $235,000, Google is offering you less guaranteed cash than Meta ($240,000). Google L6 base salaries in the Bay Area frequently reach **$250,000 \u2013 $265,000** for strong hires.\n* **Bonus:** Google L6 PM target is 20% ($47,000), compared to Meta\u2019s standard 15% target ($36,000).\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer Target)\n\n| Component | Google Initial Offer | Your Target Ask | Justification |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Exceeds Meta base ($240k) and aligns with mid-to-high band ($271k max). |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | Bridges long-term TC parity with Meta IC6 refreshes and pushes Year 1 value up. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving unvested Meta RSUs and unvested performance bonuses. |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | ~$552k \u2013 $638k | **~$680,000 \u2013 $750,000+** | Creates a compelling financial reason to switch companies. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nSchedule a call with your recruiter and deliver a calm, collaborative counter. Emphasize that you are excited about the team and role, but the current financial package does not provide enough of a premium over your established position at Meta.\n\n#### Talking Points / Script:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of this L6 role.*\n>\n> *As you know, I wasn\u2019t actively looking to leave Meta, where I\u2019m well-positioned, rated strongly, and on track with substantial unvested equity and upcoming bonus cycles. For me to make this move, the compensation needs to clearly reflect the step forward.*\n>\n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, here is where we need to be:*\n> 1. ***Base Salary:*** *$255,000 to keep it above my current base of $240,000 and aligned with the higher end of the SF band.*\n> 2. ***Sign-on Bonus:*** *$80,000 to help offset the unvested RSUs and bonus I am forfeiting by leaving my current cycle.*\n> 3. ***Equity:*** *$1.25M over 4 years to ensure long-term total comp parity with what I\u2019m tracking toward at Meta.*\n>\n> *If Google can meet these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*\n\n### Key Tips Before Speaking to the Recruiter:\n1. **Clarify the exact equity vesting schedule:** Ask explicitly: *\"Is this new-hire grant structured as 33/33/22/12, or flat 25% annual vesting?\"*\n2. **Give them the number that closes the deal:** Google recruiters work best when they can take a concrete number back to the compensation committee with the commitment: *\"If approved, the candidate will accept.\"*\n3. **Be prepared to hold your ground:** If Google refuses to move on base, push for the entire difference to be made up in **Equity** and a **First-Year Sign-on Bonus**. If they don't budge at all, staying at Meta remains the financially and professionally safer choice.",
